`IndexableSet.get(int)` (`set/IndexableSet.java:50-79` on `develop`, ee817ef) checks the index inside the loop over the elements, on each turn:

```java
for (E entry : this)
{
	if (index >= size || index < 0)   // :68
	{
		break;
	}
	if (index == count)
	{
		return entry;
	}
	count++;
}
throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("Index: " + index + ", Size: " + size);
```

The check is redundant: an index below 0 never equals `count`, and one at or above `size` is never reached, so without the check the loop runs to the end and the same exception, with the same message, follows. That makes both mutants PIT makes of line 68 equivalent, and both survive: the last run of "Mutation testing" on `develop` (run 42188169614 on ee817ef, https://github.com/astrapi69/silly-collection/actions/runs/42188169614) lists `IndexableSet` `get` 68 "changed conditional boundary" SURVIVED and "removed conditional - replaced comparison check with false" SURVIVED, two of the eleven survivors of 983 mutants. No test can kill them, as no behaviour depends on the check; it only saves the loop for a wrong index.

## Reproducing test

Passes on `develop` at ee817ef (JDK 25.0.5+8), in a copy made with `git archive`: `./gradlew --no-daemon test --tests 'io.github.astrapi69.collection.repro.*'`, 1 test of this class, 0 failures. It passes the same with line 68 removed, which is the point.

```java
package io.github.astrapi69.collection.repro;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertThrows;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

import io.github.astrapi69.collection.set.IndexableSet;

class IndexableSetGetTest
{
	@Test
	void anIndexOutsideTheSetThrowsWithOrWithoutTheCheckInTheLoop()
	{
		final IndexableSet<String> set = new IndexableSet<>();
		set.add("a");
		set.add("b");
		assertEquals("b", set.get(1));
		assertEquals("Index: -1, Size: 2",
			assertThrows(IndexOutOfBoundsException.class, () -> set.get(-1)).getMessage());
		assertEquals("Index: 2, Size: 2",
			assertThrows(IndexOutOfBoundsException.class, () -> set.get(2)).getMessage());
	}
}
```

## Precedent

- JDK 25.0.5 (`lib/src.zip`), `java/util/ArrayList.java:427-430`, `get(int)`: `Objects.checkIndex(index, size);` first, then the element. `java/util/Objects.java:345-364`, `checkIndex(int, int)`: "Checks if the `index` is within the bounds of the range from `0` (inclusive) to `length` (exclusive).", "`@throws IndexOutOfBoundsException` if the `index` is out of bounds".
- In this library, `ArrayExtensions.remove` and `removeLast` check with `Objects.checkIndex` before they work (`array/ArrayExtensions.java:815`, `:952`).

## For 29.2

Decided by the maintainer's rule of 2026-11-05: improvements, documentation additions and edge cases without a broken contract go into the next minor.

1. `get(int)` checks first, `Objects.checkIndex(index, size())`, then steps an iterator `index` times and returns the next element; no check in the loop. Removing the check then changes behaviour (a negative index would give the first element, one at `size()` a `NoSuchElementException` from the iterator), so PIT's mutants of it are killed by the existing rows of `IndexableSetTest` for -1, `size()` and `size() + 1` (`:85-87`).
2. The message becomes the JDK's, "Index -1 out of bounds for length 2", as for `ArrayExtensions.remove`. **Note** for the CHANGELOG: the message of the `IndexOutOfBoundsException` of `IndexableSet.get` is that of `Objects.checkIndex`. The alternative that keeps the message is `if (index < 0 || index >= size) throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("Index: " + index + ", Size: " + size);` before the loop; its mutants are killed the same way.
3. Check: the next run of "Mutation testing" lists no survivor in `IndexableSet`.

## Does it break callers?

Only a caller that compares the message, with point 2 as proposed. In the family no code outside this library uses `IndexableSet`: `grep -rln 'IndexableSet' --include='*.java'` over the family's checkouts finds this repository only.

